Article 22
(1) Regional Government is obligated and responsible to:
a. make policies that take into account the interests of women, especially in terms of fulfillment and protection of their rights;
b. facilitate efforts to provide services for the fulfillment of rights and provide support for facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of protection for women;
c. guarantee the protection, maintenance, and welfare of women by taking into account the rights and obligations of women;
d. supervise the implementation of protection for women;
e. conduct cross-institutional coordination among government institutions as well as with the community and the Business Sector;
f. conduct data collection and inventory of women's data;
g. provide safe houses.
(2) Regional Government has the authority to:
a. prepare standard operational guidelines for minimum service standards for efforts to fulfill rights and protection for women;
b. implement services for the fulfillment of rights and protection of women;
c. conduct supervision of efforts to fulfill women's rights and protection services for women; and
d. develop cooperation networks among government institutions as well as with Universities, the community and the Business Sector.

1.5.text:
Article 9
Forms of violence against women consist of:
a. physical violence caused by acts that result in pain, injury, wounds or disability to a person's body, miscarriage, loss of consciousness and/or causing death;
b. psychological violence caused by acts that result in fear, loss of self-confidence, loss of ability to act, feelings of helplessness and/or severe psychological suffering to a person;
c. sexual violence caused by:
1. sexual harassment;
2. sexual exploitation;
3. forced contraception;
4. forced abortion;
5. rape;
6. forced marriage;
7. forced prostitution;
8. sexual slavery;
9. sexual torture;
10. neglect.
d. Neglect caused by:
1. acts that result in the failure to fulfill the needs of women properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social;
2. acts that neglect a person within the scope of their household, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to an agreement or contract they are obligated to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/or
3. acts that result in economic dependence by means of restricting and/or prohibiting women from working properly inside or outside the home.
e. Exploitation caused by:
1. acts that exploit economically or sexually with the intention of benefiting oneself or others;
2. acts with or without the consent of the victim which include but are not limited to prostitution, forced labor or services, slavery or similar practices, oppression, extortion, utilization of physical, sexual, reproductive organs, or unlawfully removing or transplanting organs and/or body tissues or utilizing the labor or ability of a person by another party to obtain benefits both material and immaterial; and/or
3. all forms of utilization of sexual organs or other body organs of the victim to obtain benefits, and/or not limited to all activities of prostitution and indecency

Article 18
The implementation of women's empowerment in the economic field as referred to in Article 17 paragraph (2) shall be carried out by:
a. providing knowledge, skills and job training;
b. facilitating the formation of productive economic business groups;
c. facilitating the strengthening and development of productive economic business groups;
d. facilitating and providing capital assistance; and e. facilitating the development of marketing networks.

Article 5
(1) Prevention in the context of protection covers the fields of education, infrastructure, public services and spatial planning, governance and institutional management, economy, employment, social and culture;
(2) Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented through:
a. improvement of quality of life;
b. establishing and developing protection strategies and policies; and
c. establishing and developing institutional capacity related to the protection of women.

Article 11
(1) Complaint handling constitutes a series of actions carried out by integrated service providers to follow up on reports of acts of violence against women submitted by victims, families or the community.
(2) Services as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
a. conducting interviews and observation of the victim's condition;
b. making recommendations for follow-up services; c. conducting coordination and referrals to follow-up services and related parties;
d. conducting administration of the complaint process. Article 12 (1) Health services constitute efforts that include promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative aspects.
(2) Services as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
a. conducting examinations;
b. treatment;
c. follow-up care for victims;
d. conducting coordination of health rehabilitation implementation and reproductive health services;
e. conducting medico-legal examinations including collection of evidence from victims and preparation of visum et repertum.
f. conducting supporting examinations and laboratory tests on evidence.
g. conducting consultations with specialist doctors or making referrals.
h. preparing case reports.

